  "final_text": "All four steps are done.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Logs' (index `kibana_sample_data_logs`) has these fields: `@timestamp`, `timestamp`, `response` and `response.keyword`, `clientip`, `bytes`, `request`, `url`, `geo.dest`, `geo.src`, `host`, `agent`, `extension`, `machine.os`, `tags`, `message`, and a few others. The time field is `timestamp`.\n\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-6e7f9fe3 Web logs' (id `s4-6e7f9fe3-web-logs-25b01ce818cb`) has two panels:\n   - \"Requests over time\": a line chart of request count by date histogram on `timestamp`.\n   - \"Response codes\": a bar chart of request count by `response.keyword`, top 20 terms.\n\n3. **Alert rule:** 'S4-6e7f9fe3 5xx errors' (id `9c332731-e4a4-4ecd-b372-031cbe0af46e`) is an `.es-query` rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`, time field `timestamp`.\n   - It runs every 1m.\n   - The query filters `response.keyword` from \"500\" to \"599\". This is a string range, which works because all codes are 3 digits.\n   - It fires when the count is above 10 over a 5m window.\n   - It was created disabled and then enabled.\n\n4. **Panel edit:** The response-code panel is renamed to 'Responses by code'. I re-read the dashboard and the new title is shown. I read the alert rule back and it reports `enabled: true`, with status \"pending\" (it has not evaluated yet).\n\nChoices I made that you may want to change:\n- The rule's query is written against `response.keyword` rather than `response`, because the keyword field is the one that supports exact range comparisons on the code string.\n- The panel titles \"Requests over time\" and \"Response codes\" were my own labels, since the request didn't specify them. The response-code panel was renamed in step 4 as you asked.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
